 ],
 "literal": "The horse does not come, the horse does not come! / All others' horses have returned to our house; / He who gave me a son with a sparse tuft of hair, / the horse ridden by that lord does not come! / At the great confluence of two mighty rivers, / like a huge tree that lies across, / did it perish, I wonder, the horse he rode into battle?",
 "translation": "The horse does not come, the horse does not come! The horses of all others have returned to their homes. But the horse ridden by my lord, who gave me a son with a sparse tuft of hair, does not come! Like a massive tree lying across the great confluence of two mighty rivers, did it perish, I wonder, the horse he rode to confront the enemy?",
 "modern": "குதிரை வரவில்லை, குதிரை வரவில்லை! எல்லாருடைய குதிரைகளும் அவரவர் வீட்டிற்கு வந்துவிட்டன. மெல்லிய குடுமியையுடைய மகனை எனக்குத் தந்த என் தலைவன் ஏறிச் சென்ற குதிரை மட்டும் வரவில்லை! இரண்டு பெரிய ஆறுகள் இணையும் பெரிய கூடலில் குறுக்கே விழுந்து கிடக்கும் பெரிய மரத்தைப் போல, பகைவரை எதிர்த்துப் போரிட்ட அவனது குதிரை மடிந்துபோனதோ?",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Tumpai tiṇai (warfare) and depicts the theme of 'kutirai maṟam' (the valor of the horse). The wife of a warrior anxiously waits at home after a battle, noting that while everyone else's horses have returned, her husband's horse has not. She affectionately refers to her husband as the lord who gave her a young son with a soft, sparse tuft of hair. She wonders if his horse has perished in the thick of battle while standing firm against the enemy. She compares the horse to a massive tree that has fallen and lies across the great confluence of two mighty rivers, blocking their flow. This vivid image beautifully illustrates the horse's heroic stand and immense strength in the clash of two opposing armies.",
